  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nAs mentioned above, a digging trough across the Great Lakes \nwill send a weak, dry cold front into the area late Sunday, which \nwill only act to slow our weekend warmup briefly Monday. Behind this \nfront, another expansive area of surface high pressure will shove \nany appreciable moisture even further toward the Gulf, and reinforce \na dry, continental airmass across the area. This should drop \ntemperatures by roughly 5 degrees or so Monday and switch winds to \nthe north briefly, but otherwise, its effect on sensible weather \nwill be hardly noticeable. \n\nFrom Tuesday onward, though, ensemble guidance continues to suggest \nthat a broad trough across the western 2/3rds of the CONUS will \nslowly expand eastward, resulting in gradually building geopotential \nheights aloft. Meanwhile, weak south to southwest low level flow \nwill resume as the second iteration of high pressure moves eastward, \nand continues sunny skies will allow for several days of nearly full \ninsolation. As such, a steady warming trend is expected through the \nrest of the work week, and this is supported by ensemble temperature \nforecasts. Confidence is high that we will once again reach well \nabove average (50-80% above 80 degrees Wednesday onward), but we do \nnote that the NBM ensemble suite is once again a bit on the warm \nside of available guidance, and it may be the result of a slightly \noveraggressive bias correction. Still, we're talking about a \ndifference of 2-4 degrees, and at these temperature ranges this \nisn't a particularly impactful difference at these temperature \nranges.\n\nFinally, due to the continued influence of a very dry airmass and \nhigh pressure, precipitation chances are almost nonexistent \nthroughout the work week.",
